6d2882da-ca37-552c-a1de-3a04e1d44be5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407961}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59e61e3c-25e1-597c-8f73-9ab521a5d21a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186407956} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2cdbe7e-0ba6-527c-a1aa-10e0c93ae65c" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408018}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "891e3ecc-7d6f-5b4d-a28c-fc1c71a2a958" :question/math-academy-id "q-363823" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,Σ=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  select the correct options in the computations of the matrix $U$ below.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}=5$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\n\nNow, we compute the $1$st column of $U.$ It is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${u}_{1}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n|  |  | $0$ |  |  |\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal.\n\nHence, we must find vectors $u=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}]^{T}$ such that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to \n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nChoosing ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ as free variables, the general solution is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-5}} |  |  |\n|  |  | ${x}_{3}$ |  |  |\n\nthat we can rewrite as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{3}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince the two linearly independent solutions are already mutually orthogonal, we only need to normalize them. Hence, we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-6}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-7}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-8}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-9}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=5$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,-{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}={x}_{2}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{3}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables.\n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we only need to normalize them. This gives the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408164 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119811 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87dc8398-fb5c-564b-a796-db15ff30d72c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Vector Is in the Column Space of a Larger Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4543a202-3ccc-5eab-b11e-9acd9fffb1d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408141} {:answer/id #uuid "47160327-abda-5117-a3f6-534dafb7314d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408142} {:answer/id #uuid "49158a7c-f7b1-5aa2-a277-f0ac8325fc0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408143} {:answer/id #uuid "56c7eda1-7b63-555c-9f5f-b736c9f51e0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408144} {:answer/id #uuid "fa79a18c-e454-55de-b1b8-442bf38cb930" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408155}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4543a202-3ccc-5eab-b11e-9acd9fffb1d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408141} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb0ebf0b-554c-514b-a72c-5d817e3dd52a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186408161}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2009c6af-efbe-5caf-a9f3-bb999b290a0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-74221" :question/problem "The matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are given below.\nDetermine if $b\\in \\text{Col}(A).$ \nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}$, where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers such that $b={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3},$ and ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$, and ${a}_{3}$ are the respective columns of $A.$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 10 & -7 \\\\ 3 & -12 & 10\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 11 \\\\ -13\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(A),$ we need to check if the equation $Ax=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[A\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,b] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 3 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 10 & -7 & 11 \\\\ 3 & -12 & 10 & -13\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -12 & 10 & -13\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=-4 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=3 \\\\ {x}_{3} &=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=-1.$ Substituting it into the second equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{2}-(-1) & =3 \\\\ 2{x}_{2} & =2 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-4+4{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} \\\\  & =-4+4(1)-3(-1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $Ax=b$ is consistent, which implies that $b\\in \\text{Col}(A).$ \n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${a}_{1},$ ${a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 11 \\\\ -13\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}=3\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{1}}+1\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -12\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{2}}+(-1)\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{3}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=3+1+(-1)=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409634 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119858 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b72ed196-a73b-59ab-a7c3-5173249cf7e5" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of a Span"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5aaf6733-e757-5737-a6d7-d37f344e2387" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409618} {:answer/id #uuid "6536472f-623b-557c-ba85-a0b19e98cf60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409619} {:answer/id #uuid "a2ba4765-28ed-5cc7-822f-5b6db17d805d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409621} {:answer/id #uuid "f568bc4f-3c69-5e10-af38-07fcd1985ab1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409629} {:answer/id #uuid "fed87abb-5218-5d71-9132-58acdc6b2500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409630}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f568bc4f-3c69-5e10-af38-07fcd1985ab1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409629} :answer-field/id #uuid "55d04c6b-2631-5950-abad-871e8e80ece8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409632}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "06d652ec-bdc3-5e47-8845-0d191af934f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-76334" :question/problem "The vectors ${a}_{1},$ ${a}_{2},$ ${a}_{3},$ and ${a}_{4}$ are given below. Determine whether $B={{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}}.$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3},$ where ${a}_{4}={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}+{x}_{3}{a}_{3}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{4}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -22\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We want to determine if $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}}.$ So, we want to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{2}}+{x}_{3}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{3}}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -22\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{4}}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{3} &=-4 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} &=4 \\\\ 4{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3} &=-22\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 5 & -22\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 5 & -22\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 1 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -6\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the reduced matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd columns. So,  $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3},{a}_{4}}.$\nAlso, the matrix tells us that ${x}_{1}=2,$ ${x}_{2}=0,$ and ${x}_{3}=-6,$ meaning that\n\n$$\n2\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{1}}+0\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{2}}+(-6)\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{3}}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -22\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{4}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=2+0+(-6)=-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409635 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f892805c-2c2c-5105-91ba-353aefe7b7f2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Space Spanned by a Set of Vectors Using Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9829215-b974-58e4-8c44-69d894b68143" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409623} {:answer/id #uuid "c0494c3d-162e-5780-b464-32deeb834c29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409625} {:answer/id #uuid "cbf16f8a-df5e-5944-aca2-961b097f9609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409626} {:answer/id #uuid "dec9dd50-c1ab-549a-ab1c-6389d8a6d6c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409627} {:answer/id #uuid "f2d36c18-ea31-5abe-9c23-a6729a95f2b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2d36c18-ea31-5abe-9c23-a6729a95f2b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409628} :answer-field/id #uuid "71a4b4a1-6fc1-55b1-954d-d7c91a2836db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409633}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "60af0127-6c17-5939-bada-d5e4db0c560b" :question/math-academy-id "q-74970" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ and ${a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find a basis of $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ by inspection." :question/worked-solution "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{2}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=1\\cdot {a}_{1}+1\\cdot {a}_{3}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{2}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$ is spanned by just ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is ${{a}_{1},{a}_{3}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{1}},\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{3}}}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409636 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119858 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b72ed196-a73b-59ab-a7c3-5173249cf7e5" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of a Span"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d2fc535-0009-527e-8e16-197236ba5926" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409616} {:answer/id #uuid "3a46ae99-d148-5f19-94ba-416fdd09e06f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409617} {:answer/id #uuid "8e0cb693-c1d6-5a53-a944-ae0f4704df17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409620} {:answer/id #uuid "b0634bb2-64a1-5d50-8b4f-34a9ef1a9df0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409622} {:answer/id #uuid "bc6282a4-c487-5b60-9f1f-e810cbeda5e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc6282a4-c487-5b60-9f1f-e810cbeda5e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409624} :answer-field/id #uuid "065dd387-4e97-5fab-8aff-b38d1bbdcf22" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409631}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c63eedb8-35fe-5b46-b979-91cc24ab9428" :question/math-academy-id "q-76329" :question/problem "The vectors ${a}_{1},$ ${a}_{2},$ and ${a}_{3}$ are given below. Determine whether $B={{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ where ${a}_{3}={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}.$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We want to determine if $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$ So, we want to find ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in R$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{1}}+{x}_{2}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{2}}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis equation is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2} &=14 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} &=9\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the augmented matrix of the system to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 14 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 7 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the RREF matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So,  $B$ is indeed a basis of $\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}}.$\nAlso, the matrix tells us that ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=-5,$ meaning that\n\n$$\n2\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{1}}+(-5)\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{2}}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=2\\cdot (-5)=-10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409817 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119820 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e4f1675-9146-5279-9b64-07a63a9bed99" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Vector Given Its Coordinates Relative To a Given Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64a1f0ad-84e2-58dd-a8ed-11320d5d0931" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-21 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409794} {:answer/id #uuid "65c3f0eb-5935-5403-8987-9332ba2a913e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -21\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409795} {:answer/id #uuid "aae52088-4930-599a-9ee5-fd6a528c248d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-21 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409802} {:answer/id #uuid "d298d01b-b978-5454-a811-dd683962843e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -21 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409807} {:answer/id #uuid "fd15848a-b716-582c-9c1d-7cd0b9bfb17f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -21\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd15848a-b716-582c-9c1d-7cd0b9bfb17f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -21\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409811} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4638b53-3685-5dc3-a202-7169b92a21b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409814}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f44c2f3-4f54-5e93-a6f8-f1e6cfd5ee6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-35222" :question/problem "Find the vector $x$, if $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}},$ and \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix},\\,[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We know that any vector in  $\\text{Span}{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ has the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the coordinates relative to the basis $\\,B.$ Since we are told that $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\underbrace{(\\,2\\,)}_{{x}_{1}}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\underbrace{(\\,5\\,)}_{{x}_{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}= \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -25\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -21\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409819 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119859 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2926764-dfbc-5e4e-a6b0-db24a7298a64" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector Relative To a Given Basis by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2115aac5-da66-5510-8b11-176ba754f81a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409789} {:answer/id #uuid "45dbc0e6-16a9-5c62-a183-2dd30e767a3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409790} {:answer/id #uuid "60013665-0a5a-5449-9017-6cb6ae7ed2ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409792} {:answer/id #uuid "7ad6f309-f775-5604-ba46-fa9609a5688b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409798} {:answer/id #uuid "ba977b58-8add-5c57-ae69-640a36ab4ef9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60013665-0a5a-5449-9017-6cb6ae7ed2ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409792} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe323417-1cf2-5a08-b6a1-9d36f7d769a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d9b245f0-31aa-56fe-aa26-5663dd9e140f" :question/math-academy-id "q-112211" :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{b}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ then what is the value of ${x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember, the order of the vectors in a basis is important.\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{x}=\\underbrace{(\\,-8\\,)}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{b}_{1}}+\\underbrace{(\\,-7\\,)}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{b}_{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx=(-8)\\cdot {b}_{1}+(-7)\\cdot {b}_{2}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that ${x}_{2}=-7.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410713 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152061 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea9b0644-090a-52a4-bc21-18fee98acbd3" :knowledge-point/title "Date Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cf2b520-553b-53a7-88f5-8915ae76af14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Wednesday" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410590} {:answer/id #uuid "359bf237-b12e-5567-ab3b-fdf8b157a4f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Saturday" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410600} {:answer/id #uuid "625f2cc4-7141-5b58-891f-19fd378aa8fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Monday" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410623} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a5232b-17c7-57b3-9855-7a1be167543c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Friday" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410678} {:answer/id #uuid "f85803f7-5e70-5e5a-9e50-5864a6a6194a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Sunday" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410680}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7a5232b-17c7-57b3-9855-7a1be167543c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Friday" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410678} :answer-field/id #uuid "34ae9d51-f1ea-544e-a179-f5f86690c686" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "50be2be7-4190-59bf-9880-ce7373f52df0" :question/math-academy-id "q-213211" :question/problem "Jacopo Peri's Euridice, modern opera's earliest known, fully surviving work, was first performed in Florence on October 6th, 1600. Given that January 1st, 1600, was a Saturday, use congruences to determine the day of the week on which the opera was first performed.\n\n*Hint: The year 1600 was a leap year since it's an end-of-century year (multiple of $100$) and divisible by $400.$*" :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem using modular arithmetic.\n\nFirst, we note the number of complete months between January 1st, 1600, and October 1st, 1600. \n\n- There were $5$ months with $31$ days (January, March, May, July, and August).\n\n- There were $3$ months with $30$ days (April, June, and September).\n\n- There was $1$ month with $29$ days (February).\n\nTherefore, the number of days $d$ between January 1st and October 6th is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\overbrace{31(5)+30(3)+29(1)}^{\\text{days to October 1st}}+5.\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n31≡3,\\,30≡2,\\,29≡1,\\,5≡5,\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).\n$$\n\nTherefore, writing the number $d$ modulo $7,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =5(31)+3(30)+1(29)+5 \\\\  & ≡5(3)+3(2)+(1)+5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡15+6+1+5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡1+6+1+5\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡13\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡6\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the day of the week is the same as the date 6 days after January 1st, 1600, namely Friday."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410716 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152061 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea9b0644-090a-52a4-bc21-18fee98acbd3" :knowledge-point/title "Date Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45e002a6-05ef-5373-af42-302884d63b31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Thursday" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410611} {:answer/id #uuid "6f70c49a-6289-5d97-a367-f807a502fdf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Tuesday" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410630} {:answer/id #uuid "74bba275-65d4-5278-9134-c9bd06a4c526" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Monday" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410631} {:answer/id #uuid "a8c9b75b-8faf-584e-8656-ef50fe441a42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Wednesday" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410649} {:answer/id #uuid "cbd3be64-1f58-5b89-a253-861edb8875ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Friday" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f70c49a-6289-5d97-a367-f807a502fdf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Tuesday" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410630} :answer-field/id #uuid "44b86fd1-f11f-540e-b60d-259357d02f7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410690}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "700ec10d-3e3a-58fc-aadf-2fee69932797" :question/math-academy-id "q-213291" :question/problem "A parodic holiday, International Talk Like a Pirate Day, was celebrated for the first time on September 19th, 1995. Given that January 1st, 1995, was a Sunday, use congruences to determine on which day of the week this holiday was celebrated for the first time.\n\n*Hint: The year 1995 was not a leap year since it is not divisible by $4.$*" :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem using modular arithmetic.\n\nFirst, we note the number of complete months between January 1st, 1995, and September 1st, 1995. \n\n- There were $5$ months with $31$ days (January, March, May, July,  and  August).\n\n- There were $2$ months with $30$ days (April and  June).\n\n- There was $1$ month with $28$ days (February).\n\nTherefore, the number of days $d$ between January 1st and September 19th is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\overbrace{31(5)+30(2)+28(1)}^{\\text{days to September 1st}}+18.\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n31≡3,\\,30≡2,\\,28≡0,\\,18≡4\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).\n$$\n\nTherefore, writing the number $d$ modulo $7,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =5(31)+2(30)+1(28)+18 \\\\  & ≡5(3)+2(2)+1(0)+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡15+4+0+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡1+4+0+4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡9\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡2\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the day of the week is the same as the date $2$ days after January 1st, 1995, namely Tuesday."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152061 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea9b0644-090a-52a4-bc21-18fee98acbd3" :knowledge-point/title "Date Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "485aae5f-9fa1-5352-9371-7cde147454d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Sunday" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410613} {:answer/id #uuid "80321ec8-0f4c-52e6-8a67-51591491b806" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Monday" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410633} {:answer/id #uuid "b197d0a7-64fc-585e-9aa8-af38ec324fb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Wednesday" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410650} {:answer/id #uuid "fa1fd099-2e36-5b19-9d40-66a2ef4a93c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Tuesday" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410682} {:answer/id #uuid "fe06c8b2-bc20-5496-ad69-4309b87d0aab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Thursday" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410683}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa1fd099-2e36-5b19-9d40-66a2ef4a93c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Tuesday" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410682} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d016934-cf4f-5b52-99e0-61cacbf8d0d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410688}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "83693413-601d-53f0-b546-e5ed9a2ee614" :question/math-academy-id "q-213292" :question/problem "On November 16th, 1700, Spain's King Charles II died. Given that January 1st, 1700, was a Friday, use congruences to determine on which day of the week Charles II died.\n\n*Hint: The year 1700 was not a leap year since it's an end-of-century year (multiple of $100$) and is not divisible by $400.$*" :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem using modular arithmetic.\n\nFirst, we note the number of complete months between January 1st, 1700, and November 1st, 1700. \n\n- There were $6$ months with $31$ days (January, March, May, July, August, and October).\n\n- There were $3$ months with $30$ days (April, June, and September).\n\n- There was $1$ month with $28$ days (February).\n\nTherefore, the number of days $d$ between January 1st and November 16th is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\overbrace{31(6)+30(3)+28(1)}^{\\text{days to November 1st}}+15.\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n31≡3,\\,30≡2,\\,28≡0,\\,15≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).\n$$\n\nTherefore, writing the number $d$ modulo $7,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =6(31)+3(30)+1(28)+15 \\\\  & ≡6(3)+3(2)+(0)+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡18+6+0+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡4+6+0+1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡11\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡4\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the day of the week is the same as the date $4$ days after January 1st, 1700, namely Tuesday."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410723 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152061 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea9b0644-090a-52a4-bc21-18fee98acbd3" :knowledge-point/title "Date Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0435df86-3781-513b-a46f-906712e96176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Saturday" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410580} {:answer/id #uuid "4e6d9cbb-8c21-5758-abb5-f5e2a3269156" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Tuesday" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410617} {:answer/id #uuid "6337ab72-5ce7-599b-9621-a448e14bf720" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Monday" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410624} {:answer/id #uuid "69b30bc0-14ba-54bc-a56c-7790688ad638" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Sunday" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410628} {:answer/id #uuid "ef77d4b9-b779-526e-a114-59633d5da258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Thursday" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410675}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0435df86-3781-513b-a46f-906712e96176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Saturday" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410580} :answer-field/id #uuid "6264c938-98d7-59e0-8745-3f11336fceb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410694}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f453d5f9-efe9-5d81-aae2-3ab414bd5f85" :question/math-academy-id "q-213293" :question/problem "Christmas in 2100 will be celebrated, as usual, on December 25th. Given that January 1st, 2100, is a Friday, use congruences to determine the day of the week on which Christmas will be celebrated in 2100.\n\n*Hint: The year 2100 is not a leap year since it's an end-of-century year (multiple of $100$) and is not divisible by $400.$*" :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem using modular arithmetic.\n\nFirst, we note the number of complete months between January 1st, 2100, and December 1st, 2100. \n\n- There are $6$ months with $31$ days (January, March, May, July, August, and October).\n\n- There are $4$ months with $30$ days (April, June, September, and November).\n\n- There is $1$ month with $28$ days (February).\n\nTherefore, the number of days $d$ between January 1st and December 25th is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\overbrace{31(6)+30(4)+28(1)}^{\\text{days to December 1st}}+24.\n$$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n31≡3,\\,30≡2,\\,28≡0,\\,24≡3\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).\n$$\n\nTherefore, writing the number $d$ modulo $7,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =6(31)+4(30)+1(28)+24 \\\\  & ≡6(3)+4(2)+(0)+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡18+8+0+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡4+1+0+3\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡8\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\  & ≡1\\, & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the day of the week is the same as the date $1$ day after January 1st, 2100, namely Saturday."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410810 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133803 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d177b305-cdd1-5f91-b9d5-90623e52ad10" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given a QR Factorization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12a0bfb2-9ec4-5669-b98c-557ad2a9bc4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410782} {:answer/id #uuid "2af5f523-a673-51cf-8e9d-4a4edaf229e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410785} {:answer/id #uuid "6bc6960e-7f53-5f78-93da-de1e72ff8ab4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410789} {:answer/id #uuid "a27e8c2d-50df-58c3-85c9-4e38ce8d2589" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410794} {:answer/id #uuid "be9612fa-59cc-5f2d-8433-f16960b6ff97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410797}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a27e8c2d-50df-58c3-85c9-4e38ce8d2589" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410794} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a8d53f7-b5fc-5ce6-9a9c-54a7385b4356" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410806}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6e2326cd-c939-5766-9e1e-50b935159886" :question/math-academy-id "q-157095" :question/problem "Given the QR decomposition $A=QR,$ find the least-squares solution to $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nQ=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{14} & 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{14} & -3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt{14} & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{14} & 3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{14} & 3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}x & =\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{42}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{14} & \\sqrt{14} & \\sqrt{14} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3} & -3\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{{Q}^{T}}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{b} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{14} & 3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ 0 & 3\\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}x & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14}{x}_{1}+3\\sqrt{14}{x}_{2} &=-3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{3}{x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{14}{x}_{1}+3\\sqrt{14}(0) & =-3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ \\sqrt{14}{x}_{1} & =-3\\sqrt{14} \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410811 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133803 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d177b305-cdd1-5f91-b9d5-90623e52ad10" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System Given a QR Factorization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cfa9745-27c9-5c69-9259-9f33cdd30cbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410781} {:answer/id #uuid "290a80b2-d833-5e18-ba11-922da75fadbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410784} {:answer/id #uuid "84becdeb-7cb6-51f9-8247-37058f839cfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410791} {:answer/id #uuid "ab331c8e-8ec8-5a70-b8c2-e3efcd32bf48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410795} {:answer/id #uuid "d9a2f5a3-da1b-5892-8fa1-25b99c4d869f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410799}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab331c8e-8ec8-5a70-b8c2-e3efcd32bf48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410795} :answer-field/id #uuid "44672c62-c008-5400-a940-292eccf518a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c8892a71-2970-5d65-891c-76f1124825ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-139796" :question/problem "Given the QR decomposition $A=QR,$ find the least-squares solution to $Ax=b,$ where\n\n$$\nQ=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} & -1 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} & -1 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & -4\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}\\,{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & -4\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}x & =\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} & 2\\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{2} \\\\ -1 & -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{{Q}^{T}}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 9 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{b} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3\\sqrt{2} & -4\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x & =\\begin{bmatrix}38\\sqrt{2} \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}-4\\sqrt{2}{x}_{2} &=38\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2} &=-10.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=-5.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1}-4\\sqrt{2}(-5) & =38\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 3\\sqrt{2}{x}_{1} & =18\\sqrt{2} \\\\ {x}_{1} & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410856 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129214 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "88f3ba21-6407-503e-aec0-fa4462a69e73" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Using the Complex Conjugate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a822e0b-1fa5-5287-afb1-796b6d1e0af8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410833} {:answer/id #uuid "39b2aad9-d90d-5e0c-99c0-086cce1f5858" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410838} {:answer/id #uuid "b4f2b609-e44a-59ef-8d01-1bf7e729ec81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410843} {:answer/id #uuid "b6dd3430-18a3-5d4c-aa84-56246e5e6e49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410844} {:answer/id #uuid "d396ac8a-9e62-5ef7-aa22-08d3e2840f8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6dd3430-18a3-5d4c-aa84-56246e5e6e49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410844} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba30525c-f325-567a-9297-5b7c22b96813" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410853}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b89eb3d0-aaac-5e32-8106-610f887f220d" :question/math-academy-id "q-99416" :question/problem "$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\text{i} \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1+3\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\ne C,\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda }) & \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{\\lambda })\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nSo, our rotation-scaling matrix $C$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\overset{―}{\\lambda }=-1-3\\text{i},$ \n the complex conjugate of $\\lambda .$ Hence, to construct the matrix $P,$ we use the eigenvector $\\overset{―}{v},$ the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nNext, we find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{v} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\text{i} \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v})}+\\text{i}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(\\overset{―}{v}) & \\text{Im}(\\overset{―}{v}) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410857 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129134 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b049dea4-5045-57ec-ad6a-c49f30e5da1f" :knowledge-point/title "Converting a Matrix to Rotation-Scaling Form Given Multiple Parts of the Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "096e7527-4c01-5dce-8ff8-e0db3c75370d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410832} {:answer/id #uuid "0a8fe61d-12cc-5e71-bf5f-8ade179fac6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410834} {:answer/id #uuid "1c87451a-2d1c-5373-8af0-e4594174b876" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410836} {:answer/id #uuid "5113c383-2506-5331-9225-ebb1f5af24a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410840} {:answer/id #uuid "f16edcb8-7daa-5bb4-b079-ab44c7accef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410850}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f16edcb8-7daa-5bb4-b079-ab44c7accef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410850} :answer-field/id #uuid "8400b796-2f04-536c-986d-13f15ae5b122" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d6880ebc-9daa-5cd0-b5d7-2100fa039d06" :question/math-academy-id "q-99345" :question/problem "$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}-1+\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4+3\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}=C.\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1+\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411902 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d216cc0e-58a7-5fab-b5fe-60c7656338cc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Missing Numbers in the Matrix L of an LU Factorization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "356112b3-af6d-54d6-ab1f-e2dfccf01495" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411878} {:answer/id #uuid "369402a8-b0ec-5b9f-8768-00441214f23a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411879} {:answer/id #uuid "c1b1d085-a7b1-5e88-b3c0-1e9f61d1ce85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411888} {:answer/id #uuid "c3b2d7a3-ff5f-5959-ad6f-ad71afdde67b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411889} {:answer/id #uuid "fab822a6-dc94-51a0-a04d-c63e1b31e7cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411895}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fab822a6-dc94-51a0-a04d-c63e1b31e7cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411895} :answer-field/id #uuid "07cd249d-c567-51b0-aec1-9ef9737fb65f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7fabcc7a-c8df-55fd-9125-ba1a0668699c" :question/math-academy-id "q-32802" :question/problem "$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 6 & 9 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\boxed{} & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\n$$\n\nConsider the LU factorization of the matrix $A$ above. What is the value of the entry ${l}_{21}$ in the matrix $L?$" :question/worked-solution "We want to make $0$ at the position ${a}_{21}$ using Gaussian elimination. To do this, we take the $2$nd row of $A,$ and we add the $1$st row of $A$ multiplied by $-3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 6 & 9 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe entry ${l}_{21}$ of the matrix $L$ is the factor next to ${R}_{1},$ taken with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\n{l}_{21}=-(-3)=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411903 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d216cc0e-58a7-5fab-b5fe-60c7656338cc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Missing Numbers in the Matrix L of an LU Factorization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52d703ca-e917-5f3a-bb81-1adbced8e416" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411881} {:answer/id #uuid "a364db99-7dff-5c6d-a35d-72cd3c1c9979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411885} {:answer/id #uuid "b24f4893-e932-5c06-8d77-137899d5ce99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411886} {:answer/id #uuid "ed825afb-6498-5095-bfa3-a6e12cf3a09b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411890} {:answer/id #uuid "f3539b9d-f840-5d32-8b35-5249cc520f7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411892}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52d703ca-e917-5f3a-bb81-1adbced8e416" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411881} :answer-field/id #uuid "94dc230f-1164-5410-852d-f40d3f7d37e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411898}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cf67e303-e5a7-5b6d-a669-9807178d3aa4" :question/math-academy-id "q-106967" :question/problem "$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 & 6 \\\\ -35 & 14 & 50 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 7\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\boxed{} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{L}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{U}\n$$\n\nConsider the LU factorization of the matrix $A$ above. What is the value of the entry ${l}_{32}$ in the matrix $L?$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix $U$ in the LU decomposition is obtained from the original matrix $A$ by row-reducing using Gaussian elementation. In particular, to make zeros below the main diagonal, we proceed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 & 6 \\\\ -35 & 14 & 50 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-7){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we want to make $0$ at the position ${a}_{32}$ using Gaussian elimination. To do this, we take the $3$rd row of $A,$ and we add the $2$nd row of $A$ multiplied by $-1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 7\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe entry ${l}_{32}$ of the matrix $L$ is the factor next to ${R}_{2},$ taken with the opposite sign:\n\n$$\n{l}_{32}=-(-1)=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412999 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a0abad78-e492-5d09-a04d-bce54605c78c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Generalized Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "787f9f37-580f-5658-9df9-ce826b611451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472496} {:answer/id #uuid "c4403496-69e1-5d08-b02d-1b03668dd8c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "787f9f37-580f-5658-9df9-ce826b611451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472496} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f1a3fb6-945f-5879-9865-fdc5a922c3f4" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473643} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7460c1a4-dd63-595d-8732-ee34d9a2c09a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472498} {:answer/id #uuid "71f5c96a-5c5b-5605-9ce0-b480c9528139" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472499}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7460c1a4-dd63-595d-8732-ee34d9a2c09a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472498} :answer-field/id #uuid "a767f879-2f51-5809-aebb-daff65b44383" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473644} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a97ed2f-d3d1-546a-874d-42ae2f24fc40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472500} {:answer/id #uuid "be2ff147-060f-5c7b-81e0-38256805dec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472501}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be2ff147-060f-5c7b-81e0-38256805dec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472501} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3046741-713a-58b0-a1a8-53bf395bbfbb" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473645}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5e5966d3-ce4d-5835-ab91-1f478e587602" :question/math-academy-id "q-109510" :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $v$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $v$ is an eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n- **II.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $1$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n- **III.** $v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A *generalized eigenvector* ${v}_{2}$ of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda $ of a matrix $A$ is a nonzero vector such that\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-\\lambda I\\,}{\\to }0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}\\ne 0.$ Recall that an *eigenvector* is simply a generalized eigenvector of rank $1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\nFirst, we compute $A-2I:$\n\n$$\nA-2I=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the image of $v$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{w}\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Since we got a nonzero vector in the last step, we now compute the image of $w$ under the action of $A-2I:$\n\n$$\n(A-2I)w=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=0\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain the following chain of maps:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}_{v}\\overset{\\,A-2I\\,\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{w}\\overset{\\,A-2I\\,\\,}{\\to }\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{0}\n$$\n\nHere, we see that $A-2I$ maps $v$ to $0$ in $2$ steps.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$\\,$ *$v$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ of $A.$*\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415504 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133863 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ed5119c-5ce8-52dc-bd57-f9439ea199e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given a Matrix With Orthogonal Columns"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25368ac6-b32f-5b26-8c46-331ac1522f54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415487} {:answer/id #uuid "a0313793-4999-5aed-ac02-21acda57bbd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415493} {:answer/id #uuid "aff7607c-ce12-5a45-9cb9-bbc45508055e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415495} {:answer/id #uuid "ddbdf48a-0960-5123-be14-52d24536ef82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415499} {:answer/id #uuid "e6e0f3fd-8867-5cbf-95df-3e0275090b4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415500}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25368ac6-b32f-5b26-8c46-331ac1522f54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415487} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c762c76-71d9-5aca-b185-d376fcab2ad5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415503}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3591a132-7ac4-53c0-88c4-ee928e48a3a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-95943" :question/problem "$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of ${q}_{31}\\cdot {r}_{11}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{6} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{6} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{31}\\cdot {r}_{11}=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\cdot \\sqrt{6}=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415505 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133863 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ed5119c-5ce8-52dc-bd57-f9439ea199e6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given a Matrix With Orthogonal Columns"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15f4dfc0-0618-5204-93e9-55df6839e9be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415486} {:answer/id #uuid "320cae01-cb6d-546b-8fae-e515a79d9a94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415489} {:answer/id #uuid "4258535d-b0d0-5712-835e-79ab5aee8c99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415490} {:answer/id #uuid "7422fe19-732e-5581-b615-9ad03e55d8f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415492} {:answer/id #uuid "d0567542-26c9-51bb-b549-cf91536f2136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415498}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7422fe19-732e-5581-b615-9ad03e55d8f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415492} :answer-field/id #uuid "84ec1a22-6484-5de6-851b-ba1cc03d10d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415502}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b80e943-ecec-54be-8ddd-ec70be544570" :question/math-academy-id "q-96027" :question/problem "$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{q}_{11} & {q}_{12} \\\\ {q}_{21} & {q}_{22} \\\\ {q}_{31} & {q}_{32}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. Given that the columns ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ of $A$ are orthogonal, and that the columns ${q}_{1},{q}_{2}$ of $Q$ were obtained by normalizing the columns of $A,$ what is the value of ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{22}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the columns of $A$ are already orthogonal, to construct an orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we simply normalize ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{‖{a}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{9}\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{‖{a}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, pre-multiplying both sides of $A=QR$ by ${Q}^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{9} & \\frac{4}{9} & \\frac{4}{9} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{7}{9} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{9} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{9} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${q}_{22}\\cdot {r}_{22}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415506 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cd18abf3-b551-55d6-a573-690745a65b3e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a QR Factorization of a Matrix Given an Orthogonal Basis in Terms of the Columns of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c232a99-6645-5177-89dc-66f13905956a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415488} {:answer/id #uuid "713fedd8-bf09-5849-a1cc-c36eb8963d22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415491} {:answer/id #uuid "ad2e1681-8914-5558-96cf-ff55332d7ba4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415494} {:answer/id #uuid "bdaadcc3-b0d6-5daa-b301-47336aa1a8b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415496} {:answer/id #uuid "cbb4da72-b7ae-5fa0-aa7f-27b95ecda700" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbb4da72-b7ae-5fa0-aa7f-27b95ecda700" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415497} :answer-field/id #uuid "84577b17-c582-5baa-9501-36a73798f439" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415501}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a03feca5-d649-5b96-991d-3e2972b12233" :question/math-academy-id "q-95937" :question/problem "$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}{r}_{11} & {r}_{12} & {r}_{13} \\\\ 0 & {r}_{22} & {r}_{23} \\\\ 0 & 0 & {r}_{33}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}\n$$\n\nConsider the QR factorization of the matrix $A$ shown above. You're given that the system of equations given below connects the columns of $Q$ and the columns of $A.$  \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} &=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} &=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{2}+{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3} &=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{3}-{q}_{1}+2{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of ${r}_{11}+{r}_{12}+{r}_{33}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's rewrite the vectors ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of the vectors ${q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} &=\\frac{1}{3}{a}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{2} &=\\frac{1}{2}{a}_{2}+{q}_{1} \\\\ {q}_{3} &=\\frac{1}{4}{a}_{3}-{q}_{1}+2{q}_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} &=3{q}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{2} &=-2{q}_{1}+2{q}_{2} \\\\ {a}_{3} &=4{q}_{1}-8{q}_{2}+4{q}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, this system can be written in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}| & | & | \\\\ {q}_{1} & {q}_{2} & {q}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{r}_{11}+{r}_{12}+{r}_{33}=3+(-2)+4=5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186417836 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179767 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8ba09dd1-d219-5df2-adfa-d88d7b9fc481" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Point or Segment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15292884-dc42-57e9-9c54-15240ddfccc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417825} {:answer/id #uuid "73315599-47f7-5acb-b194-b9be8f07b4fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-11,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417826} {:answer/id #uuid "79732bdc-fd7e-5901-92b4-c5585d62e3b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-12,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417828} {:answer/id #uuid "94a311b2-4197-58fb-9f74-f9df05de251a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,12)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417829} {:answer/id #uuid "99809079-4b95-5960-860d-5696a69fc28e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-11,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417830}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79732bdc-fd7e-5901-92b4-c5585d62e3b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-12,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417828} :answer-field/id #uuid "69877276-8edf-5389-b9b6-59ddab5b630a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186417834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6cb153c3-10ae-52e4-a409-49d1a1150376" :question/math-academy-id "q-124153" :question/problem "Consider the transformation $T$ defined as\n\n$$\nx=4u+v-1,\\,y=-u-3v+1.\n$$\n\nLet $P(-3,1)$ be a point in the $uv$-plane. Suppose that the image of $P$ under the action of $T$ is the point ${P}^{′}$ in the $xy$-plane. What are the coordinates of ${P}^{′}?$" :question/worked-solution "An affine transformation $T:{R}^{2}\\to {R}^{2}$ is a function of the form\n\n$$\nx=Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $x,u,$ and $b$ are column vectors in ${R}^{2},$ and $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nNotice that the given transformation can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4u+v-1 \\\\ -u-3v+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4u+v \\\\ -u-3v\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}}_{A}\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}}_{u}+\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the image of $P$ under the action of $T,$ we first create a column vector $u$ containing the coordinates of $P:$ \n\n$$\nu=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nApplying $T$ to $u,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of ${P}^{′}$ are $(-12,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418992 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092191 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82830730-a9bc-57bd-9114-d0d50473ea63" :knowledge-point/title "Proving Other Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "215ee801-d946-5aef-ab70-32fd58f35dcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "less than" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418793} {:answer/id #uuid "b892634c-8fb4-5285-a9fb-27a5443f862f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "equal to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418890} {:answer/id #uuid "d7f8ac30-5290-566c-9ce4-f2f6a8c6dd0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "greater than" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418909}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b892634c-8fb4-5285-a9fb-27a5443f862f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "equal to" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418890} :answer-field/id #uuid "090d7569-bf3c-57db-948f-6d8560929d03" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418943} {:answ