d-6}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-7}}\\ge {{field-8}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-9}}={{field-10}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by the inductive hypothesis, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot {3}^{k} & \\ge {{field-11}} \\\\  & ={{field-12}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & >{{field-13}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${3}^{n}\\ge (n+1)^{2}$ is true for all\n{{field-14}}." :question/worked-solution "A variant of the principle of induction states the following: Let ${n}_{0}$ be an integer. Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P({n}_{0}),P({n}_{0}+1),P({n}_{0}+2),…,$ where\n\n- $P({n}_{0})$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every integer $k\\ge {n}_{0}.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every integer $n\\ge {n}_{0}.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{3}^{n}\\ge (n+1)^{2}\\,\\text{for}\\,n\\ge 2.\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case, $P(2).$ To do this, we substitute $n=2$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that the left-hand side is greater than the right-hand side:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=2.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{2}=9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2+1)^{2}=9,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=2.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 2.$ \n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{3}^{k}\\ge (k+1)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n{3}^{k+1}\\ge (k+2)^{2}.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is greater than the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{k+1} & ={3}^{k}\\cdot {3}^{1} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {3}^{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand by the inductive hypothesis, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot \\,\\,\\,\\,\\underbrace{{3}^{k}}_{\\ge \\,(k+1)^{2}} & \\ge 3(k+1)^{2} \\\\  & =3{k}^{2}+6k+3 \\\\  & =(k+2)^{2}+\\underbrace{2{k}^{2}+2k-1}_{>0} \\\\  & >(k+2)^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ is greater than or equal to the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${3}^{n}\\ge (n+1)^{2}$ for all $n\\ge 2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418993 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092191 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82830730-a9bc-57bd-9114-d0d50473ea63" :knowledge-point/title "Proving Other Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "290c828c-9d57-5e58-a771-3760b93af2d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n\\ge 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418802} {:answer/id #uuid "a2a95668-11e6-5c5b-b6b0-1ae8f3e35a5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418871} {:answer/id #uuid "ba82409a-ab43-5209-b2ad-a9479e89ca04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n>1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418891} {:answer/id #uuid "efb678f5-2a97-5410-88b9-ddde5e5bef72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186418929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "efb678f5-2a97-5410-88b9-ddde5e5bef72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "n\\ge 1" :db/id #edb/r