8be" :answer-field/key "IV" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454581}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5116bec0-cc13-5aa6-83e8-d90bc6884457" :question/math-academy-id "q-183415" :question/problem "$\\,$ $(m>0\\,∧n>0)⇒(m\\cdot n>0)$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the true implication above?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $m>0\\,∧n>0$ is a **necessary** condition for $m\\cdot n>0$\n- **II.** $m>0\\,∧n>0$ is a **sufficient** condition for $m\\cdot n>0$\n- **III.** $m\\cdot n>0$ is a **necessary** condition for $m>0\\,∧n>0$\n- **IV.** $m\\cdot n>0$ is a **sufficient** condition for $m>0\\,∧n>0$" :question/worked-solution "Given that the implication $S⇒N$ is true, we have the following:\n\n- $S$ being true *guarantees* that $N$ must also be true. Therefore, $S$ is a *sufficient condition* for $N.$\n\n- For $S$ to be true, it is *necessary* that $N$ is true. Therefore, $N$ is a *necessary condition* for $S.$ However, note that $N$ being true does *not* guarantee that $S$ is true.\n\nThese ideas can be summarized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\text{S}\\text{ufficient}\\,⇒\\,\\text{N}\\text{ecessary}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(\\,m>0\\,∧n>0\\,)}_{\\text{sufficient}}\\,⇒\\,\\underbrace{(\\,m\\cdot n>0\\,)}_{\\text{necessary}}\n$$\n\nis a true statement. Therefore:\n\n- Statement II is true. $m>0\\,∧n>0$ is a **sufficient** condition for $m\\cdot n>0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. $m\\cdot n>0$ is a **necessary** condition for $m>0\\,∧n>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420697 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152076 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6414abb9-00d3-55ea-a1e5-da99999c3ccc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Necessary or Sufficient Conditions From Implications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-