 an odd integer. Then, $n=\\,${{field-1}} for some integer $a.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9n-8 & ={{field-2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $b=$ {{field-3}}. Since $a$ is {{field-4}}, we have that $b$ is {{field-5}}.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9n-8 & ={{field-6}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is one larger than a multiple of $2.$ Therefore, $9n-8$ is {{field-7}}." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p.$\n\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1.$\n\nIn this case, we need to show the following:\n\n$$\nn\\text{ is odd}\\,⇒\\,9n-8\\text{ is odd}\n$$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is odd.\n\nLet $n$ be an odd integer. Then, $n=2a+1$ for some integer $a.$\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a+1$ into our expression $9n-8$ and show it is odd by writing it as a multiple of $2,$ plus $1.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9n-8 & =9(2a+1)-8 \\\\  & =18a+9-8 \\\\  & =2\\underbrace{(9a)}_{b}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we define another integer $b=9a$ (highlighted above), we have that $9n-8=2b+1,$ which shows that $9n-8$ is indeed one more than a multiple of $2.$\n\nLet $b=9a.$ Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9n-8 & =2\\underbrace{(9a)}_{b}+1 \\\\  & =2b+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is one larger than a multiple of $2.$ \n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, $9n-8$ is odd."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422741 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157255 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de2ec1dc-7365-5ba6-bc6d-6e9a13a29cd0" :knowledge-point/title "Proving a Linear Expression Is Odd"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1121103d-07e9-5063-b94e-cfeb51cc70ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422504} {:answer/id