ld/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429883}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca59627a-61c0-5ddb-824a-6032d58fc249" :question/math-academy-id "q-224166" :question/problem "What is the cube of $10?$\n\nThe cube of $10$ is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We need to evaluate ${10}^{3}.$\n\nTo evaluate ${10}^{3},$ we start by writing it out with multiplication signs:\n\n$$\n{10}^{3}=\\underbrace{10\\times 10\\times 10}_{3\\text{ times}}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the product. Since $10\\times 10=100,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{10\\times 10}_{100}\\times 10 & =100\\times 10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $100\\times 10=1,000,$ we have ${10}^{3}=1,000.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429905 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138404 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70a364f5-5384-5ba6-8142-5740aa51f4bc" :knowledge-point/title "Raising Zero or One to a Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dfc505d-98fc-5843-bf4f-e1cb78862d60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429831} {:answer/id #uuid "75c9a73e-36b6-5e3b-86b9-deb610a519cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429848} {:answer/id #uuid "84af4d0f-daf1-504b-b32f-35d8375a371c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "110" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429849} {:answer/id #uuid "ceb2c862-2582-57ba-a416-9e3fd6d2437b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "101" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429864} {:answer/id #uuid "f26dd7f4-4d26-58ba-a960-7424d6ba4984" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429869}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2dfc505d-98fc-5843-bf4f-e1cb78862d60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429831} :answer-field/id #uuid "1eba7d16-515e-5bc3-852a-c0c92e529c2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ea112c87-3b39-503f-b604-c75805eee0b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-137627" :question/problem "${0}^{11}=$" :question/worked-solution "Here, ${0}^{11}$ is in exponent form. The base is $0$ and the exponent is $11.$ \n\nThis means that $0$ is taken as a factor $11$ times. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{0}^{11}=\\underbrace{0\\times 0\\times 0\\times 0\\times 0\\times 0\\times 0\\times 0\\times 0\\times 0\\times 0}_{11\\text{ times}}=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429906 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138402 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc1a0e6d-ed51-5797-8ab7-305e861d1109" :knowledge-point/title "Cubing a Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec592311-90d1-559e-b5ba-bf63040fd157" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429868}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec592311-90d1-559e-b5ba-bf63040fd157" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429868} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4629054-af30-504c-aa6d-3ff14abcce10" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429882}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef349707-5a77-5407-8660-293dceb041c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-224181" :question/problem "What is the value of $4$ raised to the power of $3?$\n\n$4$ raised to the power of $3$ is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We need to evaluate ${4}^{3}.$\n\nTo evaluate ${4}^{3},$ we start by writing it out with multiplication signs:\n\n$$\n{4}^{3}=\\underbrace{4\\times 4\\times 4}_{3\\text{ times}}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the product. Since $4\\times 4=16,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{4\\times 4}_{16}\\times 4 & =16\\times 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we work out $16\\times 4.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,{}_{2}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $16\\times 4=64,$ we have ${4}^{3}=64.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429907 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138401 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c336cc3-c89e-5af2-b170-1fc6d9cf8307" :knowledge-point/title "Squaring a Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5791977-ff66-58e7-87e1-c6140417cf63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5791977-ff66-58e7-87e1-c6140417cf63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429860} :answer-field/id #uuid "b805c8d7-74a6-5522-a93f-640553aa32c1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f30dc369-9201-5339-acfb-6423eb9240e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-224033" :question/problem "What is the value of $9$ raised to the second power?\n\n$9$ raised to the second power is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We need to evaluate ${9}^{2}.$\n\nTo evaluate ${9}^{2},$ we write it out with multiplication signs and then compute the product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{9}^{2}=\\underbrace{9\\times 9}_{2\\text{ times}}=81\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186429908 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138402 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc1a0e6d-ed51-5797-8ab7-305e861d1109" :knowledge-point/title "Cubing a Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b855b7a-04e8-57c9-b03e-fde3abe9be3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429826} {:answer/id #uuid "3c1c1112-1448-5d43-b6b4-f23e063a47c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "243" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429832} {:answer/id #uuid "991f5f5b-9556-51c0-b887-3b809d7a026f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "946" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429853} {:answer/id #uuid "c00c01b5-b07d-5112-88b2-48115f30eb02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "618" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429859} {:answer/id #uuid "e24d96ac-ebc3-5fb5-99cb-c5d424a90e85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "729" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429866}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e24d96ac-ebc3-5fb5-99cb-c5d424a90e85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "729" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429866} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fb5ae86-36c0-5ae2-8784-a2dda7e3945b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fdc760ae-ab2b-5525-82f1-1022c98537aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-137617" :question/problem "${9}^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate ${9}^{3},$ we start by writing it out with multiplication signs:\n\n$$\n{9}^{3}=\\underbrace{9\\times 9\\times 9}_{3\\text{ times}}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the product. Since $9\\times 9=81,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{9\\times 9}_{81}\\times 9 & =81\\times 9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we work out $81\\times 9.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,{}_{}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $81\\times 9=729,$ we have ${9}^{3}=729.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430001 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119811 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87dc8398-fb5c-564b-a796-db15ff30d72c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining if a Vector Is in the Column Space of a Larger Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1828c4ca-eb5d-5389-aec1-1f80b5436666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b\\notin \\text{Col}(C)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429982} {:answer/id #uuid "33644fcb-15fc-5a07-8b9c-fcbe4057fb01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429985} {:answer/id #uuid "3bb23460-0309-50b0-9f56-6030ff2b9177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429986} {:answer/id #uuid "7c7bc9e5-99a0-5450-9994-235495344319" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429989} {:answer/id #uuid "9c2abbdd-3e2c-554e-8306-7b4a584274a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3bb23460-0309-50b0-9f56-6030ff2b9177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429986} :answer-field/id #uuid "936859a3-a75e-5199-89df-699c1acfd3b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186429998}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6e653566-d375-5ee1-b745-3dfd829381b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-111042" :question/problem "The matrix $C$ and the vector $b$ are given below.\nDetermine if $b\\in \\text{Col}(C).$ \nIn the affirmative case, find the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are real numbers such that $b={x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}+{x}_{3}{c}_{3},$ and ${c}_{1}$, ${c}_{2}$, and ${c}_{3}$ are the respective columns of $C.$\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "In order to determine whether $b$ lies in $\\text{Col}(C),$ we need to check if the equation $Cx=b$ is consistent. So, let's find the row echelon form of the augmented matrix $[C\\,|\\,b]$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[C\\,|\\,b] & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & -4 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & -8 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & ↔{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & -8 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -4 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -4 & 9\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}-4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -7 & -16 & -11\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(\\frac{7}{3}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9 & -18\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=5 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3} &=-3 \\\\ -9{x}_{3} &=-18.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get ${x}_{3}=2.$ Substituting it into the second equation gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2}+3(2) & =-3 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, from the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =5-2{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} \\\\  & =5-2(-3)-3(2) \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we have a solution, the equation $Cx=b$ is consistent, which implies that $b\\in \\text{Col}(C).$ \n\nMoreover, there is exactly one solution, so we have exactly one way to write $b$ as a linear combination of ${c}_{1},$ ${c}_{2},$ and ${c}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{b}=5\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{c}_{1}}+(-3)\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{c}_{2}}+2\\cdot \\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}}_{{c}_{3}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=5\\cdot (-3)\\cdot 2=-30.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186430359 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162045 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "566bda0f-f62d-581d-936d-baa8ae474e50" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Factor in the SVD of a Rectangular Matrix: Cases Requiring Orthogonalization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55a5e9b6-ba04-5596-85c3-98cbfa5749b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186430282} {:answer/id #uuid "8d2736ce-0ded-5e27-8be4-d1b6b00a4c03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\